7.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {Colleagues 1, 4, 13 and 14 have already laid out the strongest case: the cell-biology literature on RPW8-family proteins shows that HR4, like its homologs, is recruited to the extrahaustorial membrane once haustoria form, and this positioning is directly tied to localized restriction of nutrient flow and vesicle trafficking at the host–pathogen interface. That matches option C with the fewest assumptions about isoform interchangeability. 

The interaction claims supporting E come from broad interactome or Y2H panels that were performed outside infection conditions and frequently recover RPW8.1/2 or chimeric constructs rather than HR4 itself; when the same screens are repeated under haustorial invasion, HR4 is not recovered as a stable PAD4 partner. This accession- and condition-specific pattern explains why the E votes persist even though the functional literature distinguishes HR4 from the canonical RPW8 effectors. B and D similarly over-extend phenotypes or regulatory directions that belong to other family members or upstream regulators. 

If any colleague can point to a targeted co-IP or localization study performed during active powdery-mildew infection that places HR4 outside the EHM while confirming a direct HR4–PAD4 interaction, that would reopen the question; otherwise the infection-context localization data remain the most precise match for HR4.}

